  {
   "id": "intro",
   "type": "intro",
   "heading": {
    "en": "Yes or no, 1 or 0"
   },
   "hook": {
    "en": "Every photo, message and game on a phone is stored and processed as long strings of 0s and 1s. The decisions are made by billions of tiny switches called logic gates."
   },
   "objectives": [
    {
     "en": "Tell analogue from digital signals"
    },
    {
     "en": "Give the symbol, Boolean expression and truth table of OR, AND and NOT"
    },
    {
     "en": "Read a truth table"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "Every photo, message and game on a phone is stored and processed as long strings of 0s and 1s. The decisions are made by billions of tiny switches called logic gates."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "concept1",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Analogue and digital, and the basic gates"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "An analogue signal varies continuously; a digital signal has only two levels, called 0 (low, e.g. 0 V) and 1 (high, e.g. 5 V). A logic gate is a circuit whose output depends on its inputs by a fixed logical rule, shown in a truth table. OR: $Y = A + B$, 1 if at least one input is 1. AND: $Y = A\\cdot B$, 1 only if both are 1. NOT: $Y = \\bar A$, the opposite of the single input."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Digital: two levels, 0 and 1"
    },
    {
     "en": "OR: Y = A + B; AND: Y = A·B"
    },
    {
     "en": "NOT: Y = Ā (one input)"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "An analogue signal varies continuously; a digital signal has only two levels, called 0 (low, e.g. 0 V) and 1 (high, e.g. 5 V). A logic gate is a circuit whose output depends on its inputs by a fixed logical rule, shown in a truth table. OR: $Y = A + B$, 1 if at least one input is 1. AND: $Y = A\\cdot B$, 1 only if both are 1. NOT: $Y = \\bar A$, the opposite of the single input."
   }
  },
